<ThoughtPost
  title="A case of finding a good “career” (as “autistic”)"
  date="2025-11-21"
  description="A geometry class set a reference point my mind keeps searching for. On being a visual thinker in a verbal world, and why “do your job and follow your interests on the side” doesn’t work. An ask for serious advice, not reassurance."
>

<p>Everyone goes through this, but I suspect I carry a lot more anxiety, so I keep trying to find an answer while most people simply mature and move on. This is an attempt to get some serious advice from the reader, not reassurance.</p>

<h2>The geometry class</h2>

<p>One of the happiest memories of my childhood is a geometry class. I was in a trance state. Attention felt narrow, sustained, and effortless. Everything felt tactile and concrete. My math teacher already knew I wouldn’t disturb the class, so she let me do my thing. I was naturally attracted to tool use, the compass and ruler, and to solving visual problems.</p>

<h2>Where it kept showing up</h2>

<p>Ironically, the only subject in college for which I received a good pointer was engineering drawing, which had almost nothing to do with the rest of the curriculum built around coding and symbolic abstraction.</p>

<p>I developed an interest in carpentry as well. Again, it is tactile, deeply visual, and geometric. I could never design anything structurally sound, though.</p>

<p>I never liked learning languages, almost failed algebra in 8th grade (but later topped it when I learned about “symbolic meaning”), and never liked sports. I absolutely loved geography and absolutely hated history.</p>

<p>I have some interest in architecture, but I am terrible at it, largely because modern architectural training requires a large amount of formal, verbalized knowledge, regulation, and documentation.</p>

<h2>A visual thinker in a verbal world</h2>

<p>I work in computer vision not because I love it, but because it is the most visually grounded field among otherwise linear and abstract domains.</p>

<p>So what am I supposed to do? This resembles what cognitive psychology sometimes describes as a non verbal or visuospatial learning profile (and they call it ADHD, which is an absolutely dog shit diagnosis), where reasoning is dominated by visual, spatial, and constructive representations rather than language. Such individuals often perform actions correctly before they can explain them verbally. That is also why an architect or designer frequently struggles to describe how something will “look” using words alone.</p>

<h2>When the world valued it</h2>

<p>I envy the old timers. The Renaissance artists and visual-spatial thinkers who designed entire cathedrals and cities. Leonardo thrived in an environment where geometry, anatomy, mechanics, and drawing were not separated into artificial disciplines. The “Taj Mahal”, arguably one of the most visually refined structures in the world, was commissioned by multi-trillionaire Mughal rulers, which suggests that visual intelligence once occupied a central, socially valued role.</p>

<p>Today, this has changed dramatically. Modern technological progress is driven largely by symbolic systems: mathematics, programming languages, formal logic, and now “large language models”. While architects, artists, and visual creators still exist, they are rarely central to trillion-dollar institutions. As a result, visual thinkers are pushed toward niche roles.</p>

<h2>Why “do it on the side” fails</h2>

<p>The advice to “do your job to fill your belly and do whatever you like in your free time” does not work for visual thinkers either. I feel almost as depressed as “Devdas” when I work in finance-related roles that are entirely non visual (/statistical). No modern job is purely visual, and I can accept that. What I cannot tolerate is a job where the majority of thinking is non visual, which describes roughly 80% of contemporary tech roles.</p>

<h2>The reference point</h2>

<p>That geometry class set a reference point. The mind keeps searching for that state. Whether that search ever resolves is unclear. In short, I’m not dumb or unfocused or ADHD or immature, I’m just struggling to find something in this verbal world. Don’t want to sweat it.</p>

</ThoughtPost>
